Z||000000||fastonpumpgas||09-09-1999||12:40 PM||||Can I use anything from my 2.8 if I want to go to a 3.4 , and what about a heftier tranny, I have a 4 speed GT .. I am hopeing to get over 200 pony's after I am done||||reg||
Z||000001||PaUL||09-09-1999||03:51 PM||||I assume you me boring and stroking your 2.8 into a 3.4, this cannot be done at least safely. The cyclinder walls would be too thin.||||reg||
Z||000002||fastonpumpgas||09-09-1999||05:23 PM||||No I want to take a '92 camaro block, it should be the same as a 2.8. made with a bigger bore and differant heads to, but I am not sure if my intake will fit right, what about the motor mount ..  since it is a rear wheel drive and a fiero is considered a front wheel drive motor?<BR>||||reg||
Z||000003||PaUL||09-09-1999||06:11 PM||||If the Camaro 3.4 has cast iron heads as I think it does, it should bolt up to the stock intake manifold. The starter would have to be moved to the other side.||||reg||
Z||000004||terryk||09-09-1999||07:22 PM||||I think it's 93-95 blocks||||reg||
Z||000005||88 Fiero||09-09-1999||09:01 PM||||the 3.4 blocks found on camaro's will fit the top end of the 2.8 fiero V6. not only it fits you can use the computer without any problem..the only thing that you need is to relocate the starter...other than that everything is stock...||||reg||
Z||000006||fastonpumpgas||09-09-1999||10:08 PM||||Thanks guys or gals, i heard there is a big differance btween a 3.1 and a 3.4 in power, but is way more expensive, what is a good buy on a 3.4 longblock?  Anyone know of one, I am planning on boring it so it does matter the milage, everything is goin gto get replaced anyway....||||reg||
Z||000007||mwbackus||09-10-1999||12:25 AM||||You can get a "brand new" S-10 conversion 3.4 shortblock for less than $1700.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.sallee-chevrolet.com/PriceList/GmPerformanceEngines.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.sallee-chevrolet.com/PriceList/GmPerformanceEngines.html</A> <P>Relocation of the starter is really alot more difficult then it sounds.  I would contact Ed Parks since he has a drill jig that will enable this.  Lots of good info on this swap can be found on Ed's Fiero Factory site:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html</A> <P>and some good info on performance parts for the 3.4 here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/freddysplace/Page1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/freddysplace/Page1.html</A> <P>Mike<BR>||||reg||
Z||000008||terryk||09-10-1999||10:51 PM||||Search the web for the X-11 PROJECT. There is all of the details for remounting the starter. Also, a small part of the block must be ground off to clear and auto. Not necessary on a manual.||||reg||1
Z||000009||David||09-11-1999||11:19 PM||||In all of the topics that I read about 2.8, 3.1, 3.4 conversions the general theme is to use the iron heads from the 2.8.  Why doesn't anyone want to use the aluminum heads that come on the FWD 3.1 engines?  Never even hear them mentioned.  Must be a reason.  Can anyone enlighten me here?||||reg||1
Z||000010||batboy||09-11-1999||11:37 PM||||Actually, the aluminum heads from a 3.1 flow really well and bolt right on, but...(you knew there was going to be a but, right?) The only intake manifold that will fit is the 3.1 that goes with the heads. This means you must use the ECM and all the wiring harness that goes with it. Also, the pistons are different because the combustion chamber in the heads are different. Lots of hassles, that's why not many people mess with them.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fastonpumpgas||09-11-1999||11:38 PM||||Well david the trick is you can just change the crank , pistons , and the flywheel and presto .. you have a 3.1  .  you could use the whole 3.1 if you can get your hands on one, but the rebuild and everthing doesnt seem worth have the aluminun heads advantage, to me anyway<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000012||David||09-12-1999||12:19 PM||||I was thinking of taking the whole aluminum-headed engine, not converting an iron engine.  Guess I should have said this.||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||09-12-1999||07:57 PM||||If you decide to do the 3.1 aluminum head swap, we'll need to use the donor car's computer (ECM) and wiring harness. There are bound to be other problems too, like maybe having to use the Fiero pulleys, belts, and accessories. Often you can buy a whole Corsica or Grand Am cheaper than you can buy just an engine. Good luck, it'll be a lot of work, but I don't see why it won't work.||||reg||1
Z||000014||fastonpumpgas||09-12-1999||09:46 PM||||I think a good port job on the iron heads will be just as good, remember though.. dont mess with the vain ..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000015||Cooter||09-12-1999||09:53 PM||||On the subject of the aluminum heads- I checked out my mom's z-24 motor with the al. heads and noticed that the intake had a "freeze plug" where the distributer would be. A closer inspection revealed that a 2.8 distributer will clear the intake. The fuel injector connectors, IAC, and other intake related electronics looked like the same connectors between the fiero and cavalier motor. If this is true, would it not work to use the 2.8 distributer in the 3.1 and wire the fiero computer to the al. head intake? This would seems like the way to go. Any comments?||||reg||5
Z||000016||fastonpumpgas||09-12-1999||10:19 PM||||I am sure it is possible, but I dont think you can use the fiero intake .. if you have to ge tthe whole motor you might as weel hook the ECM up too  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||Shaun41178||09-13-1999||12:25 AM||||So you can't use the Stock Fiero upper middle or lower intake.  BIG DEAL.  If the distributor will clear and all the sensors and wiring of the fiero will mate with all the sensors of the aluminum head engien and close to the relative locations(although this doesn't really matter) I say go for it.  However I don't think the cam in the aluminum head engien has a gear on the cam to turn the distributor.  So get a new cam and then put your distributor in.  However it might have a gear on it and can be checked when the plug is pulled out.  You can alwasy have the upper intake painted Red or whatever color you want to.  Let us know what you decide to do and keep me updated for sure as I have wanted to do this as well but have always been told I would need the computer and wiring and all.  So I just figured it wasn't worth it.  however if I can use the stock Fiero wiring and computer and a few other simple things i will do it too.  Keep me posted||||reg||1
Z||000018||terryk||09-13-1999||12:47 AM||||i believe the AL head induction runs right into where the distributor would sit.<P>I would suggest, the 3.1L block/crank,  Silvo-Lite 8.8:1 pistons, and Fiero heads and induction, or a full AL 3.1L swap. The AL head 3.1L is a nice engine. <P>The wiring would take the most time. TV, beer, and the two manuals. It took me 30 hours to combine my 86 Ciera harness with the 92 3800 Supercharged harness. Not hard, just took time.<P>Consider a 90 or so 1227730 or 1227727 ECM (same, one is for under dash, other is for engine compartment).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000019||PaUL||09-13-1999||05:11 AM||||Shaun the cam must have a distributer drive gear because it also drives the oilpump. If you are going to swap in the alum. head engine ECM and all, why not use the DIS as well? ||||reg||1
Z||000020||fastonpumpgas||09-13-1999||10:48 PM||||If you want to change everything, buy a camaro ...  Dont mess with the fiero's .. i wear the fiero on my intake prowdly||||reg||1
